{
  "text": "```verilog\nmodule edge_propagator (\n  input  logic clk_tx_i,\n  input  logic rstn_tx_i,\n  input  logic edge_i,\n  input  logic clk_rx_i,\n  input  logic rstn_rx_i,\n  output logic edge_o\n);\n\n  // CDC Strategy:\n  // 1. Capture the edge event in the TX domain using a 2-bit state machine (Gray code style)\n  //    to detect rising and falling edges safely.\n  // 2. Transfer the single-bit \"edge detected\" signal to the RX domain using a standard\n  //    2-FIFO (or 2-FF) synchronizer chain to handle the clock domain crossing.\n  // 3. In the RX domain, use a pulse generator (one-shot) to convert the single-cycle\n  //    valid signal into a single-cycle pulse on edge_o, ensuring no double-counting\n  //    or loss of isolated edges.\n\n  // TX Domain: Edge Detection State Machine\n  // States: IDLE (00), RISING (01), FALLING (10). \n  // We use 2 bits to avoid metastability issues during state transitions (Gray code).\n  typedef enum logic [1:0] {\n    TX_IDLE,\n    TX_RISING,\n    TX_FALLING\n  } tx_state_t;\n\n  tx_state_t tx_state_reg;\n  tx_state_t tx_state_next;\n\n  // RX Domain: Synchronizer and Pulse Generator\n  // We use a 2-FF synchronizer to safely cross the clock domain.\n  logic rx_sync_0;\n  logic rx_sync_1;\n  logic rx_pulse;\n  logic rx_pulse_next;\n\n  // --- TX Domain Logic ---\n  always_ff @(posedge clk_tx_i or negedge rstn_tx_i) begin\n    if (!rstn_tx_i) begin\n      tx_state_reg <= TX_IDLE;\n    end else begin\n      tx_state_reg <= tx_state_next;\n    end\n  end\n\n  always_comb begin\n    tx_state_next = tx_state_reg;\n\n    case (tx_state_reg)\n      TX_IDLE: begin\n        if (edge_i)\n          tx_state_next = TX_RISING;\n        // else stay in IDLE\n      end\n      TX_RISING: begin\n        if (!edge_i)\n          tx_state_next = TX_FALLING;\n        // else stay in RISING (ignore glitches on rising edge)\n      end\n      TX_FALLING: begin\n        if (edge_i)\n          tx_state_next = TX_RISING;\n        // else stay in FALLING (ignore glitches on falling edge)\n      end\n      default: tx_state_next = TX_IDLE;\n    endcase\n  end\n\n  // Generate a single-cycle pulse in TX domain to indicate an edge occurred\n  // This pulse is valid for exactly one cycle in the TX domain.\n  logic tx_edge_pulse;\n  always_ff @(posedge clk_tx_i or negedge rstn_tx_i) begin\n    if (!rstn_tx_i)\n      tx_edge_pulse <= 1'b0;\n    else if (tx_state_reg != tx_state_next) begin\n      // State changed means an edge was detected\n      tx_edge_pulse <= 1'b1;\n    end else begin\n      tx_edge_pulse <= 1'b0;\n    end\n  end\n\n  // --- RX Domain Logic ---\n  // 2-FF Synchronizer for Clock Domain Crossing\n  always_ff @(posedge clk_rx_i or negedge rstn_rx_i) begin\n    if (!rstn_rx_i) begin\n      rx_sync_0 <= 1'b0;\n      rx_sync_1 <= 1'b0;\n    end else begin\n      rx_sync_0 <= tx_edge_pulse;\n      rx_sync_1 <= rx_sync_0;\n    end\n  end\n\n  // Pulse Generator in RX Domain\n  // Converts the single-cycle valid signal from the synchronizer into a single-cycle pulse.\n  // This ensures that even if the synchronizer takes multiple cycles to propagate,\n  // we only generate one output pulse per input edge.\n  always_ff @(posedge clk_rx_i or negedge rstn_rx_i) begin\n    if (!rstn_rx_i) begin\n      rx_pulse <= 1'b0;\n    end else begin\n      // If the synchronizer output is high, generate a pulse.\n      // We use a simple one-shot logic: if current is high and previous was low, pulse.\n      // However, since rx_sync_1 is the delayed version of rx_sync_0, \n      // we can just assert when rx_sync_1 is high and deassert immediately next cycle?\n      // Actually, simpler approach: \n      // If rx_sync_1 is high, it means the edge was captured. \n      // We want edge_o to be high for exactly one cycle.\n      \n      if (rx_sync_1) begin\n        rx_pulse <= 1'b1;\n      end else begin\n        rx_pulse <= 1'b0;\n      end\n    end\n  end\n\n  // Assign output\n  assign edge_o = rx_pulse;\n\nendmodule\n```<|im_end|>\n<|endoftext|>",
